.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Known host keys, in the format of OpenSSH's known_hosts files.
.PP
The application reads the file (this library does no I/O), loads its text, and asks about the key a server presented from its host_key callback.  What it does with the answer is its own: this module says what the file says.
.PP
.nf
.RS
UNKNOWN   nothing in the file concerns the host
MATCH     the key is the host's: a line for the host names it, or a
          certificate for the host is signed by a CA the file trusts
          for the host (a "@cert\-authority" line), is a host
          certificate in its dates and names the host
CHANGED   the file knows the host, but not by this key.  This is so
          whatever the types: a host that is known by one key, or by a
          CA, and shows another has given no reason to believe the
          second is its own
REVOKED   a "@revoked" line names the key, or the CA of a certificate
.RE
.fi
.PP
A line is "[MARKER] HOSTS KEYTYPE BASE64 [COMMENT]".  HOSTS is a comma separated list of patterns in which '*' and '?' are wildcards, a leading '!' excludes, and a name for a port other than 22 is written "[name]:port"; or one hashed name "|1|salt|hash".  Names are compared without regard to case.  A line is skipped, and counted, if it is malformed or its key is not one the policy accepts (key.h) or is a certificate: such a line cannot make a host known.  Comment lines and blank lines are not counted.
.PP
Hashed names are HMAC\-SHA\-1 of the lower\-cased name keyed with the salt. This is the one place SHA\-1 is used, and only to recognise names in files that OpenSSH writes (ssh\-keygen \-H, HashKnownHosts); it is not a signature or a key derivation, and nothing about a key depends on it.
.PP
Threading: a table is not changed by the check functions, so it may be shared by readers while nobody loads or adds.
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
