.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
int \fBgf_knownhosts_new\fP (gf_knownhosts **out);
.br
void \fBgf_knownhosts_free\fP (gf_knownhosts *kh);
.TP
int \fBgf_knownhosts_load\fP (gf_knownhosts *kh, const char *text, size_t len, size_t *bad);
Add the lines of TEXT (the contents of a known_hosts file; the table keeps no pointer into it).  *BAD, if not NULL, receives the number of lines skipped; the others are in the table.  May be called again to add another file.
.TP
int \fBgf_knownhosts_add\fP (gf_knownhosts *kh, const char *host, unsigned port, const gf_key *key);
Remember KEY as a key of HOST:PORT, as after the user accepted it, in the table only (use gf_knownhosts_format for the file).  PORT 0 is 22. KEY must not be a certificate.
.TP
enum gf_hostcheck \fBgf_knownhosts_check\fP (const gf_knownhosts *kh, const char *host, unsigned port, const gf_key *key, uint64_t now);
What the table says of KEY as the key of HOST:PORT (PORT 0 is 22).  NOW is wall time in seconds since 1970, for host certificates.
.TP
size_t \fBgf_knownhosts_sigalgs\fP (const gf_knownhosts *kh, const char *host, unsigned port, enum gf_sigalg *out, size_t cap);
The signature algorithms to ask for when connecting to HOST:PORT, so that a host known by one kind of key cannot answer with another: those of the keys the table has for it, and the certificate algorithms if it has a CA for it, in the order of policy.h.  If it knows nothing of the host, the whole default list.  Returns the number written to OUT (at most CAP), suitable for the sigalgs of gf_kex_prefs.
.TP
int \fBgf_knownhosts_format\fP (char *out, size_t cap, size_t *len, const char *host, unsigned port, const gf_key *key, int hash);
A line for a known_hosts file saying KEY is a key of HOST:PORT, ending in a newline and NUL\-terminated.  With HASH the name is hashed with a fresh salt.  KEY must not be a certificate.  *LEN receives the length without the NUL.
